    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old male named Kenneth with severe dementia has been cared for at home by his grandson for the past 4 years. The grandson reports feeling overwhelmed, missing work, and having no time for personal activities. The clinician administers the Zarit Burden Interview (ZBI), and the grandson scores 55. Which of the following is the most appropriate interpretation and next step?', '["A ZBI score \u2265 21 indicates significant caregiver burden; refer the caregiver to a social worker, recommend a caregiver support group, arrange respite care services, and screen the caregiver for depression and burnout", "The ZBI is used exclusively to assess the patient''s cognitive decline; repeat the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) to evaluate the patient''s disease progression", "Advise the caregiver that their only legal option is to continue providing full-time home care without institutional support until the patient dies", "A ZBI score of this level is within normal limits for caregivers; reassure the grandson that these feelings are completely expected and require no intervention", "A ZBI score in this range mandates immediate involuntary psychiatric hospitalization of the caregiver for acute burnout syndrome"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **Zarit Burden Interview (ZBI)** is a validated 22-item self-report tool used to assess caregiver burden in dementia caregivers. Scores range from 0–88, with higher scores indicating greater burden. Thresholds: 0–20 (little/no burden), 21–40 (mild–moderate), 41–60 (moderate–severe), 61–88 (severe burden). Any score ≥ 21 warrants clinical intervention. Interventions include: (1) referral to a licensed clinical social worker; (2) enrollment in caregiver support groups (e.g., Alzheimer''s Association); (3) arrangement of **respite care** (temporary relief: adult day programs, short-term nursing home stays); (4) depression and anxiety screening for the caregiver (PHQ-9, GAD-7); (5) community resource linkage. Caregiver health directly impacts patient outcomes. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old female named Edna with severe dementia has been cared for at home by her granddaughter for the past 7 years. The granddaughter reports feeling overwhelmed, missing work, and having no time for personal activities. The clinician administers the Zarit Burden Interview (ZBI), and the granddaughter scores 63. Which of the following is the most appropriate interpretation and next step?', '["Advise the caregiver that their only legal option is to continue providing full-time home care without institutional support until the patient dies", "A ZBI score of this level is within normal limits for caregivers; reassure the granddaughter that these feelings are completely expected and require no intervention", "A ZBI score in this range mandates immediate involuntary psychiatric hospitalization of the caregiver for acute burnout syndrome", "The ZBI is used exclusively to assess the patient''s cognitive decline; repeat the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) to evaluate the patient''s disease progression", "A ZBI score \u2265 21 indicates significant caregiver burden; refer the caregiver to a social worker, recommend a caregiver support group, arrange respite care services, and screen the caregiver for depression and burnout"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **Zarit Burden Interview (ZBI)** is a validated 22-item self-report tool used to assess caregiver burden in dementia caregivers. Scores range from 0–88, with higher scores indicating greater burden. Thresholds: 0–20 (little/no burden), 21–40 (mild–moderate), 41–60 (moderate–severe), 61–88 (severe burden). Any score ≥ 21 warrants clinical intervention. Interventions include: (1) referral to a licensed clinical social worker; (2) enrollment in caregiver support groups (e.g., Alzheimer''s Association); (3) arrangement of **respite care** (temporary relief: adult day programs, short-term nursing home stays); (4) depression and anxiety screening for the caregiver (PHQ-9, GAD-7); (5) community resource linkage. Caregiver health directly impacts patient outcomes. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old male named Richard with moderate dementia has been cared for at home by his niece for the past 5 years. The niece reports feeling overwhelmed, missing work, and having no time for personal activities. The clinician administers the Zarit Burden Interview (ZBI), and the niece scores 71. Which of the following is the most appropriate interpretation and next step?', '["The ZBI is used exclusively to assess the patient''s cognitive decline; repeat the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) to evaluate the patient''s disease progression", "Advise the caregiver that their only legal option is to continue providing full-time home care without institutional support until the patient dies", "A ZBI score \u2265 21 indicates significant caregiver burden; refer the caregiver to a social worker, recommend a caregiver support group, arrange respite care services, and screen the caregiver for depression and burnout", "A ZBI score in this range mandates immediate involuntary psychiatric hospitalization of the caregiver for acute burnout syndrome", "A ZBI score of this level is within normal limits for caregivers; reassure the niece that these feelings are completely expected and require no intervention"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **Zarit Burden Interview (ZBI)** is a validated 22-item self-report tool used to assess caregiver burden in dementia caregivers. Scores range from 0–88, with higher scores indicating greater burden. Thresholds: 0–20 (little/no burden), 21–40 (mild–moderate), 41–60 (moderate–severe), 61–88 (severe burden). Any score ≥ 21 warrants clinical intervention. Interventions include: (1) referral to a licensed clinical social worker; (2) enrollment in caregiver support groups (e.g., Alzheimer''s Association); (3) arrangement of **respite care** (temporary relief: adult day programs, short-term nursing home stays); (4) depression and anxiety screening for the caregiver (PHQ-9, GAD-7); (5) community resource linkage. Caregiver health directly impacts patient outcomes. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old female named Edith with advanced Alzheimer''s disease is brought to the clinic by her grandson, who has been the sole caregiver for 2 years. The clinician notices that the grandson appears tearful, disheveled, and states, ''I haven''t slept in days, I''ve lost 18 pounds in a month, and I feel hopeless.'' The clinician administers the PHQ-9 to the caregiver, and the score is 16. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate clinical response?', '["Tell the caregiver that mild sadness is a normal part of the caregiving role and prescribe a 2-week course of diphenhydramine to help them sleep", "Immediately place the patient in a nursing home without discussing the family''s preferences, as this is the most efficient way to resolve the caregiver''s stress", "Recognize that the caregiver is experiencing major depressive disorder; refer immediately for psychiatric evaluation and treatment, arrange emergency respite care for the patient, and conduct a safety assessment including suicide risk screening", "Advise the caregiver to take a one-day vacation from their responsibilities, then return to full caregiving duties the following day", "Document the caregiver''s score in the patient''s medical record as evidence of secondary self-neglect and file a report with Adult Protective Services"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Caregiver depression** is a major public health concern: up to 40–50% of dementia caregivers develop clinical depression. A **PHQ-9 ≥ 10** indicates moderate-to-severe depression requiring evaluation and treatment. The clinician''s response must be tripartite: (1) **Treat the caregiver**: refer for psychiatric evaluation (therapy and/or pharmacotherapy); (2) **Relieve the burden**: arrange emergency/crisis respite care so the patient is safely supervised while the caregiver gets urgent help; (3) **Safety**: perform a suicide risk assessment, as caregiver depression is associated with suicidal ideation and even caregiver-patient homicide. Dismissing or minimizing caregiver psychological distress is a clinical failure that endangers both parties. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old female named Martha with advanced Alzheimer''s disease is brought to the clinic by her granddaughter, who has been the sole caregiver for 4 years. The clinician notices that the granddaughter appears tearful, disheveled, and states, ''I haven''t slept in days, I''ve lost 15 pounds in a month, and I feel hopeless.'' The clinician administers the PHQ-9 to the caregiver, and the score is 18. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate clinical response?', '["Recognize that the caregiver is experiencing major depressive disorder; refer immediately for psychiatric evaluation and treatment, arrange emergency respite care for the patient, and conduct a safety assessment including suicide risk screening", "Tell the caregiver that mild sadness is a normal part of the caregiving role and prescribe a 2-week course of diphenhydramine to help them sleep", "Document the caregiver''s score in the patient''s medical record as evidence of secondary self-neglect and file a report with Adult Protective Services", "Immediately place the patient in a nursing home without discussing the family''s preferences, as this is the most efficient way to resolve the caregiver''s stress", "Advise the caregiver to take a one-day vacation from their responsibilities, then return to full caregiving duties the following day"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Caregiver depression** is a major public health concern: up to 40–50% of dementia caregivers develop clinical depression. A **PHQ-9 ≥ 10** indicates moderate-to-severe depression requiring evaluation and treatment. The clinician''s response must be tripartite: (1) **Treat the caregiver**: refer for psychiatric evaluation (therapy and/or pharmacotherapy); (2) **Relieve the burden**: arrange emergency/crisis respite care so the patient is safely supervised while the caregiver gets urgent help; (3) **Safety**: perform a suicide risk assessment, as caregiver depression is associated with suicidal ideation and even caregiver-patient homicide. Dismissing or minimizing caregiver psychological distress is a clinical failure that endangers both parties. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old male named Bernard with advanced Alzheimer''s disease is brought to the clinic by his spouse, who has been the sole caregiver for 6 years. The clinician notices that the spouse appears tearful, disheveled, and states, ''I haven''t slept in days, I''ve lost 10 pounds in a month, and I feel hopeless.'' The clinician administers the PHQ-9 to the caregiver, and the score is 20. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate clinical response?', '["Tell the caregiver that mild sadness is a normal part of the caregiving role and prescribe a 2-week course of diphenhydramine to help them sleep", "Document the caregiver''s score in the patient''s medical record as evidence of secondary self-neglect and file a report with Adult Protective Services", "Immediately place the patient in a nursing home without discussing the family''s preferences, as this is the most efficient way to resolve the caregiver''s stress", "Advise the caregiver to take a one-day vacation from their responsibilities, then return to full caregiving duties the following day", "Recognize that the caregiver is experiencing major depressive disorder; refer immediately for psychiatric evaluation and treatment, arrange emergency respite care for the patient, and conduct a safety assessment including suicide risk screening"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Caregiver depression** is a major public health concern: up to 40–50% of dementia caregivers develop clinical depression. A **PHQ-9 ≥ 10** indicates moderate-to-severe depression requiring evaluation and treatment. The clinician''s response must be tripartite: (1) **Treat the caregiver**: refer for psychiatric evaluation (therapy and/or pharmacotherapy); (2) **Relieve the burden**: arrange emergency/crisis respite care so the patient is safely supervised while the caregiver gets urgent help; (3) **Safety**: perform a suicide risk assessment, as caregiver depression is associated with suicidal ideation and even caregiver-patient homicid