    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old male named Henry with advanced Alzheimer''s disease is brought to the clinic by his paid caregiver, who has been the sole caregiver for 4 years. The clinician notices that the paid caregiver appears tearful, disheveled, and states, ''I haven''t slept in days, I''ve lost 10 pounds in a month, and I feel hopeless.'' The clinician administers the PHQ-9 to the caregiver, and the score is 15. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate clinical response?', '["Immediately place the patient in a nursing home without discussing the family''s preferences, as this is the most efficient way to resolve the caregiver''s stress", "Advise the caregiver to take a one-day vacation from their responsibilities, then return to full caregiving duties the following day", "Document the caregiver''s score in the patient''s medical record as evidence of secondary self-neglect and file a report with Adult Protective Services", "Tell the caregiver that mild sadness is a normal part of the caregiving role and prescribe a 2-week course of diphenhydramine to help them sleep", "Recognize that the caregiver is experiencing major depressive disorder; refer immediately for psychiatric evaluation and treatment, arrange emergency respite care for the patient, and conduct a safety assessment including suicide risk screening"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Caregiver depression** is a major public health concern: up to 40–50% of dementia caregivers develop clinical depression. A **PHQ-9 ≥ 10** indicates moderate-to-severe depression requiring evaluation and treatment. The clinician''s response must be tripartite: (1) **Treat the caregiver**: refer for psychiatric evaluation (therapy and/or pharmacotherapy); (2) **Relieve the burden**: arrange emergency/crisis respite care so the patient is safely supervised while the caregiver gets urgent help; (3) **Safety**: perform a suicide risk assessment, as caregiver depression is associated with suicidal ideation and even caregiver-p