    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old male named Robert with advanced Alzheimer''s disease is brought to the clinic by his spouse, who has been the sole caregiver for 8 years. The clinician notices that the spouse appears tearful, disheveled, and states, ''I haven''t slept in days, I''ve lost 20 pounds in a month, and I feel hopeless.'' The clinician administers the PHQ-9 to the caregiver, and the score is 11. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate clinical response?', '["Immediately place the patient in a nursing home without discussing the family''s preferences, as this is the most efficient way to resolve the caregiver''s stress", "Recognize that the caregiver is experiencing major depressive disorder; refer immediately for psychiatric evaluation and treatment, arrange emergency respite care for the patient, and conduct a safety assessment including suicide risk screening", "Document the caregiver''s score in the patient''s medical record as evidence of secondary self-neglect and file a report with Adult Protective Services", "Tell the caregiver that mild sadness is a normal part of the caregiving role and prescribe a 2-week course of diphenhydramine to help them sleep", "Advise the caregiver to take a one-day vacation from their responsibilities, then return to full caregiving duties the following day"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Caregiver depression** is a major public health concern: up to 40–50% of dementia caregivers develop clinical depression. A **PHQ-9 ≥ 10** indicates moderate-to-severe depression requiring evaluation and treatment. The clinician''s response must be tripartite: (1) **Treat the caregiver**: refer for psychiatric evaluation (therapy and/or pharmacotherapy); (2) **Relieve the burden**: arrange emergency/crisis respite care so the patient is safely supervised while the caregiver gets urgent help; (3) **Safety**: perform a suicide risk assessment, as caregiver depression is associated with suicidal ideation and even caregiver-patient homicide. Dismissing or minimizing caregiver psychological distress is a clinical failure that endangers both parties. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old male named Leonard with advanced Alzheimer''s disease is brought to the clinic by his grandson, who has been the sole caregiver for 7 years. The clinician notices that the grandson appears tearful, disheveled, and states, ''I haven''t slept in days, I''ve lost 12 pounds in a month, and I feel hopeless.'' The clinician administers the PHQ-9 to the caregiver, and the score is 12. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate clinical response?', '["Recognize that the caregiver is experiencing major depressive disorder; refer immediately for psychiatric evaluation and treatment, arrange emergency respite care for the patient, and conduct a safety assessment including suicide risk screening", "Document the caregiver''s score in the patient''s medical record as evidence of secondary self-neglect and file a report with Adult Protective Services", "Tell the caregiver that mild sadness is a normal part of the caregiving role and prescribe a 2-week course of diphenhydramine to help them sleep", "Advise the caregiver to take a one-day vacation from their responsibilities, then return to full caregiving duties the following day", "Immediately place the patient in a nursing home without discussing the family''s preferences, as this is the most efficient way to resolve the caregiver''s stress"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Caregiver depression** is a major public health concern: up to 40–50% of dementia caregivers develop clinical depression. A **PHQ-9 ≥ 10** indicates moderate-to-severe depression requiring evaluation and treatment. The clinician''s response must be tripartite: (1) **Treat the caregiver**: refer for psychiatric evaluation (therapy and/or pharmacotherapy); (2) **Relieve the burden**: arrange emergency/crisis respite care so the patient is safely supervised while the caregiver gets urgent help; (3) **Safety**: perform a suicide risk assessment, as caregiver depression is associated with suicidal ideation and even caregiver-patient homicide. Dismissing or minimizing caregiver psychological distress is a clinical failure that endangers both parties. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old male named Vernon with advanced Alzheimer''s disease is brought to the clinic by his adult son, who has been the sole caregiver for 3 years. The clinician notices that the adult son appears tearful, disheveled, and states, ''I haven''t slept in days, I''ve lost 20 pounds in a month, and I feel hopeless.'' The clinician administers the PHQ-9 to the caregiver, and the score is 14. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate clinical response?', '["Advise the caregiver to take a one-day vacation from their responsibilities, then return to full caregiving duties the following day", "Immediately place the patient in a nursing home without discussing the family''s preferences, as this is the most efficient way to resolve the caregiver''s stress", "Tell the caregiver that mild sadness is a normal part of the caregiving role and prescribe a 2-week course of diphenhydramine to help them sleep", "Document the caregiver''s score in the patient''s medical record as evidence of secondary self-neglect and file a report with Adult Protective Services", "Recognize that the caregiver is experiencing major depressive disorder; refer immediately for psychiatric evaluation and treatment, arrange emergency respite care for the patient, and conduct a safety assessment including suicide risk screening"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Caregiver depression** is a major public health concern: up to 40–50% of dementia caregivers develop clinical depression. A **PHQ-9 ≥ 10** indicates moderate-to-severe depression requiring evaluation and treatment. The clinician''s response must be tripartite: (1) **Treat the caregiver**: refer for psychiatric evaluation (therapy and/or pharmacotherapy); (2) **Relieve the burden**: arrange emergency/crisis respite care so the patient is safely supervised while the caregiver gets urgent help; (3) **Safety**: perform a suicide risk assessment, as caregiver depression is associated with suicidal ideation and even caregiver-patient homicide. Dismissing or minimizing caregiver psychological distress is a clinical failure that endangers both parties. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old female named Grace with advanced Alzheimer''s disease is brought to the clinic by her adult son, who has been the sole caregiver for 8 years. The clinician notices that the adult son appears tearful, disheveled, and states, ''I haven''t slept in days, I''ve lost 20 pounds in a month, and I feel hopeless.'' The clinician administers the PHQ-9 to the caregiver, and the score is 11. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate clinical response?', '["Advise the caregiver to take a one-day vacation from their responsibilities, then return to full caregiving duties the following day", "Immediately place the patient in a nursing home without discussing the family''s preferences, as this is the most efficient way to resolve the caregiver''s stress", "Recognize that the caregiver is experiencing major depressive disorder; refer immediately for psychiatric evaluation and treatment, arrange emergency respite care for the patient, and conduct a safety assessment including suicide risk screening", "Tell the caregiver that mild sadness is a normal part of the caregiving role and prescribe a 2-week course of diphenhydramine to help them sleep", "Document the caregiver''s score in the patient''s medical record as evidence of secondary self-neglect and file a report with Adult Protective Services"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Caregiver depression** is a major public health concern: up to 40–50% of dementia caregivers develop clinical depression. A **PHQ-9 ≥ 10** indicates moderate-to-severe depression requiring evaluation and treatment. The clinician''s response must be tripartite: (1) **Treat the caregiver**: refer for psychiatric evaluation (therapy and/or pharmacotherapy); (2) **Relieve the burden**: arrange emergency/crisis respite care so the patient is safely supervised while the caregiver gets urgent help; (3) **Safety**: perform a suicide risk assessment, as caregiver depression is associated with suicidal ideation and even caregiver-patient homicide. Dismissing or minimizing caregiver psychological distress is a clinical failure that endangers both parties. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 81-year-old male named George with advanced Alzheimer''s disease is brought to the clinic by his granddaughter, who has been the sole caregiver for 8 years. The clinician notices that the granddaughter appears tearful, disheveled, and states, ''I haven''t slept in days, I''ve lost 12 pounds in a month, and I feel hopeless.'' The clinician administers the PHQ-9 to the caregiver, and the score is 16. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate clinical response?', '["Document the caregiver''s score in the patient''s medical record as evidence of secondary self-neglect and file a report with Adult Protective Services", "Immediately place the patient in a nursing home without discussing the family''s preferences, as this is the most efficient way to resolve the caregiver''s stress", "Tell the caregiver that mild sadness is a normal part of the caregiving role and prescribe a 2-week course of diphenhydramine to help them sleep", "Recognize that the caregiver is experiencing major depressive disorder; refer immediately for psychiatric evaluation and treatment, arrange emergency respite care for the patient, and conduct a safety assessment including suicide risk screening", "Advise the caregiver to take a one-day vacation from their responsibilities, then return to full caregiving duties the following day"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Caregiver depression** is a major public health concern: up to 40–50% of dementia caregivers develop clinical depression. A **PHQ-9 ≥ 10** indicates moderate-to-severe depression requiring evaluation and treatment. The clinician''s response must be tripartite: (1) **Treat the caregiver**: refer for psychiatric evaluation (therapy and/or pharmacotherapy); (2) **Relieve the burden**: arrange emergency/crisis respite care so the patient is safely supervised while the caregiver gets urgent help; (3) **Safety**: perform a suicide risk assessment, as caregiver depression is associated with suicidal ideation and even caregiver-patient homicide. Dismissing or minimizing caregiver psychological distress is a clinical failure that endangers both parties. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old female named Florence with moderate dementia lives at home. Her adult son reports severe burnout and states they desperately need a 2-week break to attend to their own health. The patient is medically stable with no acute illness but requires assistance with all 6 basic activities of daily living (ADLs). The family asks about care options during this period. Which of the following represents the most appropriate form of ''respite care'' for this situation?', '["Advise the adult son that respite care is only covered by private insurance and is not available through Medicare, Medicaid, or community programs", "Suggest that the family reduce the patient''s caloric intake to induce mild sedation and reduce the caregiver''s supervision burden", "Instruct the caregiver to leave the patient home alone with daily phone check-ins, as the patient is medically stable", "Recommend that the patient be admitted to the hospital for a 2-week ''social admission'' while the caregiver rests", "Arrange a short-term temporary placement in a skilled nursing facility (SNF) or a specialized dementia respite program, which provides supervised care for the patient while the caregiver takes a planned break"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Respite care** is defined as temporary, substitute care provided to give family caregivers a planned, structured break from their caregiving responsibilities. Types include: (1) **In-home respite**: a trained volunteer or paid aide comes to the home to supervise the patient; (2) **Adult Day Health Programs (ADHP)**: structured community-based daytime programs (cognitive stimulation, meals, socialization, nursing monitoring); (3) **Short-term residential respite**: temporary placement in a licensed care facility (SNF, memory care unit) for days to weeks. Medicare Part A covers short-term SNF stays after a qualifying hospital admission. The National Family Caregiver Support Program (Older Americans Act) provides respite funding. Hospitalizing a stable patient solely for social reasons is inappropriate and costly. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
