ble patient solely for social reasons is inappropriate and costly. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old female named Evelyn with moderate dementia lives at home. Her daughter reports severe burnout and states they desperately need a 2-week break to attend to their own health. The patient is medically stable with no acute illness but requires assistance with all 6 basic activities of daily living (ADLs). The family asks about care options during this period. Which of the following represents the most appropriate form of ''respite care'' for this situation?', '["Recommend that the patient be admitted to the hospital for a 2-week ''social admission'' while the caregiver rests", "Arrange a short-term temporary placement in a skilled nursing facility (SNF) or a specialized dementia respite program, which provides supervised care for the patient while the caregiver takes a planned break", "Suggest that the family reduce the patient''s caloric intake to induce mild sedation and reduce the caregiver''s supervision burden", "Instruct the caregiver to leave the patient home alone with daily phone check-ins, as the patient is medically stable", "Advise the daughter that respite care is only covered by private insurance and is not available through Medicare, Medicaid, or community programs"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Respite care** is defined as temporary, substitute care provided to give family caregivers a planned, structured break from their caregiving responsibilities. Types include: (1) **In-home respite**: a trained volunteer or paid aide comes to the home to supervise the patient; (2) **Adult Day Health Programs (ADHP)**: structured community-based daytime programs (cognitive stimulation, meals, socialization, nursing monitoring); (3) **Short-term residential respite**: temporary placement in a licensed care facility (SNF, memory care unit) for days to weeks. Medicare Part A covers short-term SNF stays after a qualifying hospital admission. The National Family Caregiver Support Program (Older Americans Act) provides respite funding. Hospitalizing a stable patient solely for social reasons is inappropriate and costly. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old male named Donald with mild-to-moderate dementia lives at home with his working granddaughter. The granddaughter must work 14 hours per day and cannot provide daytime supervision. The patient wanders, forgets to eat, and has had two minor kitchen injuries. The patient remains ambulatory, social, and does not have complex medical needs requiring continuous nursing care. Which of the following is the most appropriate community-based care recommendation?', '["Prescribe a daily dose of lorazepam 2 mg to chemically suppress the wandering behavior during the caregiver''s working hours", "Arrange immediate permanent placement in a locked memory care nursing facility, as any degree of wandering requires 24-hour institutionalization", "Enroll the patient in an Adult Day Health Program (ADHP), which provides structured daytime supervision, cognitive stimulation, meals, socialization, and safety monitoring while allowing the patient to return home in the evening", "Recommend the patient wear a medical alert necklace and be left home alone with the stove disconnected as a safety measure", "Advise the granddaughter to quit their job and provide full-time home care, as this is the most appropriate standard of care for patients with wandering behaviors"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Adult Day Health Programs (ADHP)** are community-based, structured programs that operate during daytime hours (typically 8 AM–5 PM) and provide: (1) supervised activities and cognitive stimulation; (2) nutritional meals and hydration monitoring; (3) socialization with peers; (4) basic health monitoring (vital signs, medication management); (5) caregiver respite. Eligibility: ambulatory or semi-ambulatory older adults with cognitive or functional impairments who are safe to return home in the evening. Benefits: delays nursing home placement, reduces caregiver burden, improves patient quality of life. Funding: Medicaid HCBS waivers, Older Americans Act grants, private pay. Using benzodiazepines for behavioral control is dangerous (Beers Criteria) and constitutes a chemical restraint. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old female named Irene with mild-to-moderate dementia lives at home with her working spouse. The spouse must work 22 hours per day and cannot provide daytime supervision. The patient wanders, forgets to eat, and has had two minor kitchen injuries. The patient remains ambulatory, social, and does not have complex medical needs requiring continuous nursing care. Which of the following is the most appropriate community-based care recommendation?', '["Arrange immediate permanent placement in a locked memory care nursing facility, as any degree of wandering requires 24-hour institutionalization", "Prescribe a daily dose of lorazepam 2 mg to chemically suppress the wandering behavior during the caregiver''s working hours", "Enroll the patient in an Adult Day Health Program (ADHP), which provides structured daytime supervision, cognitive stimulation, meals, socialization, and safety monitoring while allowing the patient to return home in the evening", "Recommend the patient wear a medical alert necklace and be left home alone with the stove disconnected as a safety measure", "Advise the spouse to quit their job and provide full-time home care, as this is the most appropriate standard of care for patients with wandering behaviors"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Adult Day Health Programs (ADHP)** are community-based, structured programs that operate during daytime hours (typically 8 AM–5 PM) and provide: (1) supervised activities and cognitive stimulation; (2) nutritional meals and hydration monitoring; (3) socialization with peers; (4) basic health monitoring (vital signs, medication management); (5) caregiver respite. Eligibility: ambulatory or semi-ambulatory older adults with cognitive or functional impairments who are safe to return home in the evening. Benefits: delays nursing home placement, reduces caregiver burden, improves patient quality of life. Funding: Medicaid HCBS waivers, Older Americans Act grants, private pay. Using benzodiazepines for behavioral control is dangerous (Beers Criteria) and constitutes a chemical restraint. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
