l control is dangerous (Beers Criteria) and constitutes a chemical restraint. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old female named Esther with severe dementia is cared for at home. You learn that the primary caregiver is the patient''s 6-year-old grandson, who is also managing their own chronic conditions including Type 2 diabetes and hypertension. The grandson reports missing their own medical appointments and medications. Which of the following describes this phenomenon and the most appropriate clinical action?', '["The caregiver''s missed appointments are solely the responsibility of their own primary care physician and do not fall within the scope of the geriatrician''s clinical duties", "File a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) immediately, as the caregiver''s health management failure constitutes medical neglect of the patient", "Advise the caregiver to manage their own health conditions using over-the-counter medications to avoid the time burden of medical appointments", "This represents ''caregiver role strain,'' where the caregiver''s own health is compromised by caregiving demands; the clinician must assess both patient and caregiver needs, connect the caregiver to community support services, and ensure the caregiver maintains their own medical care", "Recommend that both the patient and the caregiver be admitted to a skilled nursing facility simultaneously to provide coordinated care for both"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Caregiver role strain** is defined as the physical, emotional, and health burden experienced by informal caregivers who sacrifice their own well-being to care for a dependent family member. It is especially prevalent in **older spousal caregivers** or adult children with their own chronic medical conditions. Consequences include: missed medical appointments, medication non-adherence, worsening chronic disease, depression, social isolation, and increased mortality. The clinician caring for the older patient must adopt a **dyadic (patient + caregiver) care model**: (1) screen for caregiver health status; (2) encourage the caregiver to maintain their own medical care; (3) connect to community resources (Meals on Wheels, PACE, community health workers); (4) arrange additional home support. This is a core principle in the **Geriatric Assessment** framework. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old female named Ruth with severe dementia is cared for at home. You learn that the primary caregiver is the patient''s 8-year-old grandson, who is also managing their own chronic conditions including Type 2 diabetes and hypertension. The grandson reports missing their own medical appointments and medications. Which of the following describes this phenomenon and the most appropriate clinical action?', '["The caregiver''s missed appointments are solely the responsibility of their own primary care physician and do not fall within the scope of the geriatrician''s clinical duties", "This represents ''caregiver role strain,'' where the caregiver''s own health is compromised by caregiving demands; the clinician must assess both patient and caregiver needs, connect the caregiver to community support services, and ensure the caregiver maintains their own medical care", "File a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) immediately, as the caregiver''s health management failure constitutes medical neglect of the patient", "Recommend that both the patient and the caregiver be admitted to a skilled nursing facility simultaneously to provide coordinated care for both", "Advise the caregiver to manage their own health conditions using over-the-counter medications to avoid the time burden of medical appointments"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Caregiver role strain** is defined as the physical, emotional, and health burden experienced by informal caregivers who sacrifice their own well-being to care for a dependent family member. It is especially prevalent in **older spousal caregivers** or adult children with their own chronic medical conditions. Consequences include: missed medical appointments, medication non-adherence, worsening chronic disease, depression, social isolation, and increased mortality. The clinician caring for the older patient must adopt a **dyadic (patient + caregiver) care model**: (1) screen for caregiver health status; (2) encourage the caregi