    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old male named Arthur with severe dementia is cared for at home. You learn that the primary caregiver is the patient''s 5-year-old paid caregiver, who is also managing their own chronic conditions including Type 2 diabetes and hypertension. The paid caregiver reports missing their own medical appointments and medications. Which of the following describes this phenomenon and the most appropriate clinical action?', '["File a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) immediately, as the caregiver''s health management failure constitutes medical neglect of the patient", "Advise the caregiver to manage their own health conditions using over-the-counter medications to avoid the time burden of medical appointments", "This represents ''caregiver role strain,'' where the caregiver''s own health is compromised by caregiving demands; the clinician must assess both patient and caregiver needs, connect the caregiver to community support services, and ensure the caregiver maintains their own medical care", "Recommend that both the patient and the caregiver be admitted to a skilled nursing facility simultaneously to provide coordinated care for both", "The caregiver''s missed appointments are solely the responsibility of their own primary care physician and do not fall within the scope of the geriatrician''s clinical duties"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Caregiver role strain** is defined as the physical, emotional, and health burden experienced by informal caregivers who sacrifice their own well-being to care for a dependent family member. It is especially prevalent in **older spousal caregivers** or adult children with their own chronic medical conditions. Consequences include: missed medical appointments, medication non-adherence, worsening chronic disease, depression, social isolation, and increased mortality. The clinician caring for the older patient must adopt a **dyadic (patient + caregiver) care model**: (1) screen for caregiver health status; (2) encourage the caregiver to maintain their own medical care; (3) connect to community resources (Meals on Wheels, PACE, community health workers); (4) arrange additional home support. This is a core principle in the **Geriatric Assessment** framework. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old female named Eleanor with severe dementia is cared for at home. You learn that the primary caregiver is the patient''s 8-year-old adult son, who is also managing their own chronic conditions including Type 2 diabetes and hypertension. The adult son reports missing their own medical appointments and medications. Which of the following describes this phenomenon and the most appropriate clinical action?', '["This represents ''caregiver role strain,'' where the caregiver''s own health is compromised by caregiving demands; the clinician must assess both patient and caregiver needs, connect the caregiver to community support services, and ensure the caregiver maintains their own medical care", "Recommend that both the patient and the caregiver be admitted to a skilled nursing facility simultaneously to provide coordinated care for both", "The caregiver''s missed appointments are solely the responsibility of their own primary care physician and do not fall within the scope of the geriatrician''s clinical duties", "Advise the caregiver to manage their own health conditions using over-the-counter medications to avoid the time burden of medical appointments", "File a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) immediately, as the caregiver''s health management failure constitutes medical neglect of the patient"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Caregiver role strain** is defined as the physical, emotional, and health burden experienced by informal caregivers who sacrifice their own well-being to care for a dependent family member. It is especially prevalent in **older spousal caregivers** or adult children with their own chronic medical conditions. Consequences include: missed medical appointments, medication non-adherence, worsening chronic disease, depression, social isolation, and increased mortality. The clinician caring for the older patient must adopt a **dyadic (patient + caregiver) care model**: (1) screen for caregiver health status; (2) encourage the caregiver to maintain their own medical care; (3) connect to community resources (Meals on Wheels, PACE, community health workers); (4) arrange additional home support. This is a core principle in the **Geriatric Assessment** framework. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old female named Ruby is dually eligible for Medicare and Medicaid. She has multiple chronic conditions (diabetes, COPD, mild dementia), requires assistance with 4 ADLs, and meets nursing home level of care criteria. However, Ruby and her family strongly prefer to remain in the community. A social worker mentions a program that provides comprehensive medical, social, and supportive services in a community setting. Which program is being described?', '["The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit, which provides a single-day comprehensive health assessment but does not include ongoing community-based services", "Medicare Part D Extra Help (Low Income Subsidy), which provides prescription drug cost assistance for low-income Medicare beneficiaries", "Program of All-inclusive Care for the Elderly (PACE), which provides comprehensive interdisciplinary team-based care (medical, social, rehabilitative, and supportive services) through an adult day health center to nursing home-eligible individuals who choose to remain in the community", "Medicaid Managed Long-Term Services and Supports (MLTSS), which exclusively provides nursing home care and excludes all community-based alternatives", "The Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP), which provides monthly food vouchers for older adults with nutritional risk factors"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **Program of All-inclusive Care for the Elderly (PACE)** is a federally recognized, capitated managed care program that provides comprehensive medical and social services to individuals who: (1) are ≥55 years old; (2) are certified as needing **nursing home level of care**; (3) are able to safely live in the community with PACE support; (4) are enrolled in Medicare and/or Medicaid. PACE provides an **interdisciplinary team** (physician, nurse, social worker, PT, OT, dietitian, activity coordinator) through an adult day health center plus in-home services as needed. PACE accepts full financial risk and coordinates ALL care. PACE delays or prevents nursing home placement and improves quality of life. It is considered the gold standard for community-based long-term care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4)'),
