tes ALL care. PACE delays or prevents nursing home placement and improves quality of life. It is considered the gold standard for community-based long-term care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old male named George is dually eligible for Medicare and Medicaid. He has multiple chronic conditions (diabetes, COPD, mild dementia), requires assistance with 6 ADLs, and meets nursing home level of care criteria. However, George and his family strongly prefer to remain in the community. A social worker mentions a program that provides comprehensive medical, social, and supportive services in a community setting. Which program is being described?', '["Medicaid Managed Long-Term Services and Supports (MLTSS), which exclusively provides nursing home care and excludes all community-based alternatives", "The Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP), which provides monthly food vouchers for older adults with nutritional risk factors", "Program of All-inclusive Care for the Elderly (PACE), which provides comprehensive interdisciplinary team-based care (medical, social, rehabilitative, and supportive services) through an adult day health center to nursing home-eligible individuals who choose to remain in the community", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit, which provides a single-day comprehensive health assessment but does not include ongoing community-based services", "Medicare Part D Extra Help (Low Income Subsidy), which provides prescription drug cost assistance for low-income Medicare beneficiaries"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **Program of All-inclusive Care for the Elderly (PACE)** is a federally recognized, capitated managed care program that provides comprehensive medical and social services to individuals who: (1) are ≥55 years old; (2) are certified as needing **nursing home level of care**; (3) are able to safely live in the community with PACE support; (4) are enrolled in Medicare and/or Medicaid. PACE provides an **interdisciplinary team** (physician, nurse, social worker, PT, OT, dietitian, activity coordinator) through an adult day health center plus in-home services as needed. PACE accepts full financial risk and coordinates ALL care. PACE delays or prevents nursing home placement and improves quality of life. It is considered the gold standard for community-based long-term care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old male named Albert is discharged home after a hip fracture repair. He needs assistance with bathing, dressing, and meal preparation (personal care ADLs), but has no wound care, IV therapy, or skilled therapy needs. The family asks what type of home-based assistance is most appropriate. Which of the following best describes the correct service?', '["A home health aide (HHA) or personal care aide (PCA), who provides non-skilled, custodial assistance with personal care activities of daily living (ADLs) such as bathing, dressing, grooming, and meal preparation", "A Medicare-certified home health agency providing skilled nursing visits, as Medicare Part A covers all home assistance services for patients discharged from the hospital", "A licensed clinical social worker (LCSW) for daily home visits to provide psychotherapy during the post-discharge recovery period", "A durable medical equipment (DME) supplier to provide a hospital-grade bed and mechanical lifting device as the primary service", "A hospice home care team, which is appropriate for any patient who is discharged from the hospital with functional limitations"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Home health aides (HHAs)** and **personal care aides (PCAs)** provide **custodial (non-skilled) care**: bathing, dressing, grooming, toileting, meal preparation, light housekeeping, and companionship. This differs critically from **skilled home health** (Medicare Part A/B covered): skilled nursing (wound care, IV antibiotics, medication teaching), physical therapy, occupational therapy, speech therapy — which require a physician''s order, homebound status, and a specific skilled need. **Medicare does NOT cover custodial-only home care** (long-term bathing/dressing assistance). Custodial home care is funded by: Medicaid HCBS waivers, private pay, or long-term care insurance. Understanding this distinction is clinically critical for discharge planning and patient/family counseling. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old male named Fred is discharged home after a hip fracture repair. He needs assistance with bathing, dressing, and meal preparation (personal care ADLs), but has no wound care, IV therapy, or skilled therapy needs. The family asks what type of home-based assistance is most appropriate. Which of the following best describes the correct service?', '["A home health aide (HHA) or personal care aide (PCA), who provides non-skilled, custodial assistance with personal care activities of daily living (ADLs) such as bathing, dressing, grooming, and meal preparation", "A licensed clinical social worker (LCSW) for daily home visits to provide psychotherapy during the post-discharge recovery period", "A hospice home care team, which is appropriate for any patient who is discharged from the hospital with functional limitations", "A durable medical equipment (DME) supplier to provide a hospital-grade bed and mechanical lifting device as the primary service", "A Medicare-certified home health agency providing skilled nursing visits, as Medicare Part A covers all home assistance services for patients discharged from the hospital"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Home health aides (HHAs)** and **personal care aides (PCAs)** provide **custodial (non-skilled) care**: bathing, dressing, grooming, toileting, meal preparation, light housekeeping, and companionship. This differs critically from **skilled home health** (Medicare Part A/B covered): skilled nursing (wound care, IV antibiotics, medication teaching), physical therapy, occupational therapy, speech therapy — which require a physician''s order, homebound status, and a specific skilled need. **Medicare does NOT cover custodial-only home care** (long-term bathing/dressing assistance). Custodial home care is funded by: Medicaid HCBS waivers, private pay, or long-term care insurance. Understanding this distinction is clinically critical for discharge planning and patient/family counseling. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Ger