    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old female named Eleanor is discharged home after a hip fracture repair. She needs assistance with bathing, dressing, and meal preparation (personal care ADLs), but has no wound care, IV therapy, or skilled therapy needs. The family asks what type of home-based assistance is most appropriate. Which of the following best describes the correct service?', '["A home health aide (HHA) or personal care aide (PCA), who provides non-skilled, custodial assistance with personal care activities of daily living (ADLs) such as bathing, dressing, grooming, and meal preparation", "A hospice home care team, which is appropriate for any patient who is discharged from the hospital with functional limitations", "A durable medical equipment (DME) supplier to provide a hospital-grade bed and mechanical lifting device as the primary service", "A licensed clinical social worker (LCSW) for daily home visits to provide psychotherapy during the post-discharge recovery period", "A Medicare-certified home health agency providing skilled nursing visits, as Medicare Part A covers all home assistance services for patients discharged from the hospital"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Home health aides (HHAs)** and **personal care aides (PCAs)** provide **custodial (non-skilled) care**: bathing, dressing, grooming, toileting, meal preparation, light housekeeping, and companionship. This differs critically from **skilled home health** (Medicare Part A/B covered): skilled nursing (wound care, IV antibiotics, medication teaching), physical therapy, occupational therapy, speech therapy — which require a physician''s order, homebound status, and a specific skilled need. **Medicare does NOT cover custodial-only home care** (long-term bathing/dressing assistance). Custodial home care is funded by: Medicaid HCBS waivers, private pay, or long-term care insurance. Understanding this distinction is clinically critical for discharge planning and patient/family counseling. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old male named David is discharged home after a hip fracture repair. He needs assistance with bathing, dressing, and meal preparation (personal care ADLs), but has no wound care, IV therapy, or skilled therapy needs. The family asks what type of home-based assistance is most appropriate. Which of the following best describes the correct service?', '["A Medicare-certified home health agency providing skilled nursing visits, as Medicare Part A covers all home assistance services for patients discharged from the hospital", "A durable medical equipment (DME) supplier to provide a hospital-grade bed and mechanical lifting device as the primary service", "A home health aide (HHA) or personal care aide (PCA), who provides non-skilled, custodial assistance with personal care activities of daily living (ADLs) such as bathing, dressing, grooming, and meal preparation", "A licensed clinical social worker (LCSW) for daily home visits to provide psychotherapy during the post-discharge recovery period", "A hospice home care team, which is appropriate for any patient who is discharged from the hospital with functional limitations"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Home health aides (HHAs)** and **personal care aides (PCAs)** provide **custodial (non-skilled) care**: bathing, dressing, grooming, toileting, meal preparation, light housekeeping, and companionship. This differs critically from **skilled home health** (Medicare Part A/B covered): skilled nursing (wound care, IV antibiotics, medication teaching), physical therapy, occupational therapy, speech therapy — which require a physician''s order, homebound status, and a specific skilled need. **Medicare does NOT cover custodial-only home care** (long-term bathing/dressing assistance). Custodial home care is funded by: Medicaid HCBS waivers, private pay, or long-term care insurance. Understanding this distinction is clinically critical for discharge planning and patient/family counseling. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Ge