    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old male named Clarence with advanced dementia is cared for at home by his paid caregiver. During a home health visit, the nurse notes the patient appears poorly groomed and has lost 18 lbs. The paid caregiver states: ''Some days I think about just disappearing or ending things for both of us so we can both be at peace.'' Which of the following is the most critical immediate clinical action?', '["Advise the family to reduce the patient''s food intake to minimize caregiver preparation burden, then reassess in 3 months", "Reassure the caregiver that such thoughts are universally experienced by all long-term caregivers and require no immediate clinical response", "Document the caregiver''s statement as a metaphorical expression of frustration and take no further action at this visit", "Recognize this as a potential caregiver-patient violence/homicide-suicide risk; immediately assess lethality, ensure the patient''s safety, involve crisis mental health services for the caregiver, and report to Adult Protective Services (APS)", "Prescribe the caregiver a one-week supply of zolpidem 5 mg to improve sleep and schedule a follow-up in 6 weeks"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Caregiver statements expressing a wish to ''end things for both of us'' represent a **homicide-suicide ideation** — a recognized and documented risk among severely burned-out caregivers. This is a **psychiatric emergency**. Clinical steps: (1) **Immediate safety assessment**: evaluate lethality, access to weapons/medications; (2) **Patient safety**: ensure immediate supervisory safety for the cognitively impaired patient; (3) **Crisis mental health referral**: emergency psychiatric evaluation for the caregiver; (4) **Adult Protective Services (APS) report**: mandated reporting for patient vulnerability; (5) **Emergency respite care** placement. The combination of patient weight loss and caregiver suicidal/homicidal ideation suggests the patient may already be at risk of neglect or harm. This is one of the most critical clinical scenarios in geriatric social medicine. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
