    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old male named Edward with advanced dementia is cared for at home by his paid caregiver. During a home health visit, the nurse notes the patient appears poorly groomed and has lost 22 lbs. The paid caregiver states: ''Some days I think about just disappearing or ending things for both of us so we can both be at peace.'' Which of the following is the most critical immediate clinical action?', '["Reassure the caregiver that such thoughts are universally experienced by all long-term caregivers and require no immediate clinical response", "Advise the family to reduce the patient''s food intake to minimize caregiver preparation burden, then reassess in 3 months", "Prescribe the caregiver a one-week supply of zolpidem 5 mg to improve sleep and schedule a follow-up in 6 weeks", "Document the caregiver''s statement as a metaphorical expression of frustration and take no further action at this visit", "Recognize this as a potential caregiver-patient violence/homicide-suicide risk; immediately assess lethality, ensure the patient''s safety, involve crisis mental health services for the caregiver, and report to Adult Protective Services (APS)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Caregiver statements expressing a wish to ''end things for both of us'' represent a **homicide-suicide ideation** — a recognized and documented risk among severely burned-out caregivers. This is a **psychiatric emergency**. Clinical steps: (1) **Immediate safety assessment**: evaluate lethality, access to weapons/medications; (2) **Patient safety**: ensure immediate supervisory safety for the cognitively impaired patient; (3) **Crisis mental health referral**: emergency psychiatric evaluation for the caregiver; (4) **Adult Protective Services (APS) report**: mandated reporting for patient vulnerability; (5) **Emergency respite care** placement. The combination of patient weight loss and caregiver suicidal/homicidal ideation suggests the patient may already be at risk of neglect or harm. This is one of the most critical clinical scenarios in geriatric social medicine. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old female named Martha with moderate dementia is cared for at home by her grandson. The family has no private insurance for home care services and asks the clinician what publicly funded support programs exist for family caregivers. Which of the following accurately describes a federally funded program specifically designed to support informal family caregivers of older adults?', '["The American Geriatrics Society (AGS) operates a national network of free 24/7 respite care centers funded entirely by private donations", "Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI) automatically reimburses caregivers at a rate of $15 per hour for all hours spent providing care to a disabled family member", "The National Family Caregiver Support Program (NFCSP), authorized under Title III-E of the Older Americans Act, provides caregivers with information, assistance, counseling, respite care, and supplemental services through local Area Agencies on Aging (AAA)", "The Patient Self-Determination Act mandates that all hospitals provide 40 hours of free respite care per month to family caregivers of hospitalized patients", "Medicare Part B''s ''Caregiver Benefit'' provides direct monthly payments to family members who care for Medicare beneficiaries at home as a substitute for institutional care"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **National Family Caregiver Support Program (NFCSP)** is a federal program (Title III-E of the **Older Americans Act, 2000**) administered by the Administration for Community Living (ACL) through state **Area Agencies on Aging (AAA)**. It provides 5 core services: (1) **Information** about available services; (2) **Assistance** in accessing support services; (3) **Individual counseling**, organization of support groups, and training; (4) **Respite care** (temporary relief for caregivers); (5) **Supplemental services** (limited, to complement care). Eligibility: adult family caregivers of individuals ≥ 60 years, or grandparents caring for grandchildren. Medicare does NOT pay caregivers directly. The AAA is the local gateway to virtually all community aging services. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
