    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old female named Marie with moderate dementia is cared for at home by her paid caregiver. The family has no private insurance for home care services and asks the clinician what publicly funded support programs exist for family caregivers. Which of the following accurately describes a federally funded program specifically designed to support informal family caregivers of older adults?', '["The American Geriatrics Society (AGS) operates a national network of free 24/7 respite care centers funded entirely by private donations", "Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI) automatically reimburses caregivers at a rate of $15 per hour for all hours spent providing care to a disabled family member", "The Patient Self-Determination Act mandates that all hospitals provide 40 hours of free respite care per month to family caregivers of hospitalized patients", "Medicare Part B''s ''Caregiver Benefit'' provides direct monthly payments to family members who care for Medicare beneficiaries at home as a substitute for institutional care", "The National Family Caregiver Support Program (NFCSP), authorized under Title III-E of the Older Americans Act, provides caregivers with information, assistance, counseling, respite care, and supplemental services through local Area Agencies on Aging (AAA)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **National Family Caregiver Support Program (NFCSP)** is a federal program (Title III-E of the **Older Americans Act, 2000**) administered by the Administration for Community Living (ACL) through state **Area Agencies on Aging (AAA)**. It provides 5 core services: (1) **Information** about available services; (2) **Assistance** in accessing support services; (3) **Individual counseling**, organization of support groups, and training; (4) **Respite care** (temporary relief for caregivers); (5) **Supplemental services** (limited, to complement care). Eligibility: adult family caregivers of individuals ≥ 60 years, or grandparents caring for grandchildren. Medicare does NOT pay caregivers directly. The AAA is the local gateway to virtually all community aging services. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old male named Clarence with advanced dementia requires 24-hour supervision. His daughter has quit their job to provide full-time care and the family''s income has dropped to $800/month Social Security. The daughter reports they cannot afford adult diapers, nutritional supplements, or medication copays. What is the most appropriate primary clinical intervention to address this financial crisis?', '["Refer to a licensed social worker for a comprehensive financial eligibility assessment including Medicaid (for long-term services), Supplemental Security Income (SSI), Medicare Extra Help, the Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program (LIHEAP), and local Area Agency on Aging benefits counseling", "Advise the family to take out a second mortgage on their home to fund the care costs, as this is the standard expected financial sacrifice for long-term caregiving", "Immediately place the patient in a nursing home as the sole solution to the financial crisis without assessing eligibility for community-based Medicaid programs", "Tell the family that until they spend down all assets to Medicaid eligibility thresholds, no public assistance programs are available to them", "Prescribe generic versions of all medications only and discharge the family with no further social support"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Financial toxicity** of caregiving is a major social determinant of health. The average out-of-pocket cost of caring for a dementia patient at home exceeds $47,000/year. A geriatric social worker must assess eligibility for: (1) **Medicaid HCBS waivers** — fund home-based personal care aides, adult day programs, respite care; (2) **Supplemental Security Income (SSI)** — income support for low-income older adults; (3) **Medicare Extra Help/Low Income Subsidy** — reduces Part D drug costs; (4) **Area Agency on Aging (AAA)** — Meals on Wheels, transportation, caregiver support; (5) **LIHEAP** — energy bill assistance; (6) **State Pharmaceutical Assistance Programs (SPAP)** — drug cost reduction. Immediate nursing home placement without assessing community-based Medicaid options deprives the family of preferred and often less expensive alternatives. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old male named Earl with advanced dementia requires 24-hour supervision. His paid caregiver has quit their job to provide full-time care and the family''s income has dropped to $950/month Social Security. The paid caregiver reports they cannot afford adult diapers, nutritional supplements, or medication copays. What is the most appropriate primary clinical intervention to address this financial crisis?', '["Prescribe generic versions of all medications only and discharge the family with no further social support", "Immediately place the patient in a nursing home as the sole solution to the financial crisis without assessing eligibility for community-based Medicaid programs", "Advise the family to take out a second mortgage on their home to fund the care costs, as this is the standard expected financial sacrifice for long-term caregiving", "Tell the family that until they spend down all assets to Medicaid eligibility thresholds, no public assistance programs are available to them", "Refer to a licensed social worker for a comprehensive financial eligibility assessment including Medicaid (for long-term services), Supplemental Security Income (SSI), Medicare Extra Help, the Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program (LIHEAP), and local Area Agency on Aging benefits counseling"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Financial toxicity** of caregiving is a major social determinant of health. The average out-of-pocket cost of caring for a dementia patient at home exceeds $47,000/year. A geriatric social worker must assess eligibility for: (1) **Medicaid HCBS waivers** — fund home-based personal care aides, adult day programs, respite care; (2) **Supplemental Security Income (SSI)** — income support for low-income older adults; (3) **Medicare Extra Help/Low Income Subsidy** — reduces Part D drug costs; (4) **Area Agency on Aging (AAA)** — Meals on Wheels, transportation, caregiver support; (5) **LIHEAP** — energy bill assistance; (6) **State Pharmaceutical Assistance Programs (SPAP)** — drug cost reduction. Immediate nursing home placement without assessing community-based Medicaid options deprives the family of preferred and often less expensive alternatives. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old male named Lawrence with advanced dementia requires 24-hour supervision. His nephew has quit their job to provide full-time care and the family''s income has dropped to $880/month Social Security Disability. The nephew reports they cannot afford adult diapers, nutritional supplements, or medication copays. What is the most appropriate primary clinical intervention to address this financial crisis?', '["Tell the family that until they spend down all assets to Medicaid eligibility thresholds, no public assistance programs are available to them", "Refer to a licensed social worker for a comprehensive financial eligibility assessment including Medicaid (for long-term services), Supplemental Security Income (SSI), Medicare Extra Help, the Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program (LIHEAP), and local Area Agency on Aging benefits counseling", "Immediately place the patient in a nursing home as the sole solution to the financial crisis without assessing eligibility for community-based Medicaid programs", "Advise the family to take out a second mortgage on their home to fund the care costs, as this is the standard expected financial sacrifice for long-term caregiving", "Prescribe generic versions of all medications only and discharge the family with no further social support"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Financial toxicity** of caregiving is a major social determinant of health. The average out-of-pocket cost of caring for a dementia patient at home exceeds $47,000/year. A geriatric social worker must assess eligibility for: (1) **Medicaid HCBS waivers** — fund home-based personal care aides, adult day programs, respite care; (2) **Supplemental Security Income (SSI)** — income support for low-income older adults; (3) **Medicare Extra Help/Low Income Subsidy** — reduces Part D drug costs; (4) **Area Agency on Aging (AAA)** — Meals on Wheels, transportation, caregiver support; (5) **LIHEAP** — energy bill assistance; (6) **State Pharmaceutical Assistance Programs (SPAP)** — drug cost reduction. Immediate nursing home placement without assessing community-based Medicaid options deprives the family of preferred and often less expensive alternatives. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old female named Beatrice with advanced dementia requires 24-hour supervision. Her paid caregiver has quit their job to provide full-time care and the family''s income has dropped to $1,100/month combined income. The paid caregiver reports they cannot afford adult diapers, nutritional supplements, or medication copays. What is the most appropriate primary clinical intervention to address this financial crisis?', '["Advise the family to take out a second mortgage on their home to fund the care costs, as this is the standard expected financial sacrifice for long-term caregiving", "Immediately place the patient in a nursing home as the sole solution to the financial crisis without assessing eligibility for community-based Medicaid programs", "Refer to a licensed social worker for a comprehensive financial eligibility assessment including Medicaid (for long-term services), Supplemental Security Income (SSI), Medicare Extra Help, the Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program (LIHEAP), and local Area Agency on Aging benefits counseling", "Tell the family that until they spend down all assets to Medicaid eligibility thresholds, no public assistance programs are available to them", "Prescribe generic versions of all medications only and discharge the family with no further social support"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Financial toxicity** of caregiving is a major social determinant of health. The average out-of-pocket cost of caring for a dementia patient at home exceeds $47,000/year. A geriatric social worker must assess eligibility for: (1) **Medicaid HCBS waivers** — fund home-based personal care aides, adult day programs, respite care; (2) **Supplemental Security Income (SSI)** — income support for low-income older adults; (3) **Medicare Extra Help/Low Income Subsidy** — reduces Part D drug costs; (4) **Area Agency on Aging (AAA)** — Meals on Wheels, transportation, caregiver support; (5) **LIHEAP** — energy bill assistance; (6) **State Pharmaceutical Assistance Programs (SPAP)** — drug cost reduction. Immediate nursing home placement without assessing community-based Medicaid options deprives the family of preferred and often less expensive alternatives. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4)'),
